{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790888590,"nonce":"TspvfxzLOyQwQizGH4AQOz9HtPo=","title":"A Finding cannot propose anything, so noise reduction and Incident membership still depend on a human noticing","message":"**What is wrong.** ADR 0053 §2 and 0052 §2: a Suggestion proposes a change to oto's own configuration (e.g. a policy's count condition) or to an Incident's membership; a human applies it or it lapses; there is no reject verb.\n\n**Where.** Suggestion rows tied to a Finding; an apply action that performs the ordinary policy or membership edit with \"suggested by Investigation X\" provenance; lapse time. Because a Case belongs to at most one Incident (ADR 0052 §4), a membership Suggestion for a Case already in another Incident applies as a **move** (`b2672a1`), and says so before it is applied.\n\n**Scope.** medium.\n\n**Done when.** Applying a count-condition Suggestion edits the policy exactly as a human edit would, with provenance; applying a membership Suggestion adds the Case — or moves it, if it is in another Incident — with the applier as actor; no decline or dismiss endpoint exists (`dismiss` is banned); unapplied Suggestions lapse and stop showing.\n\n**Blocked by.** `180a525`, `b2672a1`\n\n**Mode.** AFK — can be implemented and merged without a human decision. Source: ADR 0052/0053/0054 design session, 2026-10-02.","files":null}]}